<div class="desk-intro-details">
<p class="small">No account or upload. Plain .docx files up to 8 MiB. Recover embedded PNG and JPEG files before importing; numbered placeholders help place them in WordPress. Need a .md file instead? <a href="/word-to-markdown/">Convert Word to Markdown</a>.</p>
<p id="sample" class="small">No document handy? <a id="download-word-practice" href="/samples/word-to-markdown.docx" download="drafttopage-fictional-sample.docx">Download a fictional Word sample</a>, then choose that file above. Or <button id="start-sample" type="button" class="secondary">Try a fictional sample</button> to explore the editor only. The button's sample is prewritten, not a Word-file import.</p>
<details id="word-practice"><summary>Practice the complete handoff without publishing</summary>
<p>This fictional .docx is generated by Draft to Page, not captured from a current Microsoft Word session. It lets you exercise file import instead of loading prewritten editor text. It contains no images, private comments, or tracked changes, so it does not test those features.</p>
<ol id="word-practice-steps">
<li><strong>Keep existing work safe.</strong> Download a private workspace backup before replacing an article. Download the fictional Word sample above, then use the existing Word file control to choose it. Inspect the conversion notices and preview before applying anything.</li>
<li><strong>Compare the sample.</strong> Use the expected content below and the original file. The title should move to its separate field. After reviewing the import, choose <strong>Import and continue to export</strong>. If the page asks about replacing current writing, proceed only after saving what you need.</li>
<li><strong>Approve this export separately.</strong> Compare the article preview again, inspect the WordPress handoff findings, and check the export review box. Choose <strong>Copy WordPress blocks</strong>. Wait for the page to confirm the copy; a failed or stale copy is not ready to paste.</li>
<li><strong>Save an unpublished WordPress draft.</strong> Keep this page open and use a separate tab for a WordPress site where you may create a test draft. Create a new empty post, enter the separate title, click the empty paragraph in Visual editor, and paste with Ctrl+V or Command+V. Inspect the result, then save as a draft. Do not publish this exercise.</li>
<li><strong>Close, reopen, and edit.</strong> Close the post editor and reopen the saved draft. Compare every item below. Confirm that headings, list items, and table cells remain editable without unexpected-content warnings. Change one ordinary word, save again, and reopen once more to check that your edit survived. Keep the post unpublished.</li>
</ol>
<h2 id="word-practice-expected">Expected sample content</h2>
<p>Compare content and editable structure, not fonts or page layout. Literal punctuation may be escaped in the intermediate Markdown. The expected WordPress body has one H2 heading, one paragraph, one numbered list, and one table; the post title is separate.</p>
<div class="article-table" role="region" aria-labelledby="word-practice-expected" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col">Expected result before your deliberate edit</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Separate post title</th><td>A small publishing plan</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">H2 heading</th><td>Before publishing</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Paragraph and link</th><td>Keep <strong>clear structure</strong> and source links. The words <strong>clear structure</strong> are bold. The source links label points to <code>https://example.com/</code>; you do not need to open it.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Numbered list</th><td>1. Review the article.<br>2. Preview the destination.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">One two-column table</th><td>Header: Step | Status.<br>First data row: Review | Ready.<br>Last data row: Publish | Planned.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>If markup appears as literal text, undo that paste or use another empty draft and follow the <a href="/help/publishing-handoff/#wordpress-code-editor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code editor fallback (new tab)</a>. If a heading, number, link, table cell, or edit is missing, keep the draft unpublished and compare it with the original. Do not treat clicking Copy or checking a box as successful publishing.</p>
<p class="small">This is a manual practice guide, not an automated WordPress check. Draft to Page does not read your WordPress post or record these results. Passing this small generated sample does not establish compatibility with your own Word documents, images, plugins, or theme. Save important writing explicitly before leaving this page.</p>
</details>
